window.BLOG_ARTICLE_CONTENT = window.BLOG_ARTICLE_CONTENT || {};
window.BLOG_ARTICLE_CONTENT["en/vln-weekly-2026-10-17"] = {"body": "\n<aside aria-label=\"Translation status\" class=\"translation-note\">\n<p>English translation of the <a data-local-language=\"zh\" href=\"#/post/vln-weekly-2026-10-17\" lang=\"zh-CN\">Chinese article</a> revised 2026-10-17. Translation updated 2026-10-17.</p>\n<p>Companion articles labeled <span class=\"language-label\">Chinese only</span> are available in Chinese. English editions will be added as they are completed.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"key-conclusions\">1. Key conclusions</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Continuous VLN gains must be considered alongside collisions.</strong> Of 175 new works, 5 explicitly involve R2R-CE; 4 abstracts provide related numbers, while VLN-AIEP only names the benchmark. <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.15782v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">RSI-Nav</a> reports R2R-CE SR of 68.6%; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14682v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">VLN-Safe</a> finds contact in 38.5–56.8% of successful episodes when sliding after collisions is allowed. Our assessment: report success and safety separately, and distinguish continuous R2R-CE from discrete R2R.</li>\n<li><strong>Memory must maintain trustworthy state and govern execution.</strong> <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.18478v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">MemoryVLN</a> studies spatial grounding and cross-context reuse; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19431v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">S3-Nav</a> separates persistent state, decision-facing evidence, and execution authority; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21586v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">AVALON</a> distinguishes human permissions from robot-observed traversability. Our assessment: test evidence withdrawal and completion verification, beyond memory capacity.</li>\n<li><strong>Long-horizon failures emerge at stage handoffs.</strong> <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.18307v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">I2O2I-Nav</a> raises full-mission success from 1.15% to 4.02%, still far below primitive performance; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.17089v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">MultINav</a> incorporates connectivity and visibility changes caused by opening doors and containers. Our assessment: starting each stage at its predecessor’s actual endpoint exposes difficulties hidden by isolated subtask evaluation.</li>\n<li><strong>Self-improvement increasingly targets diagnosed failures.</strong> <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19420v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">WEDGE</a> generates environments around policy weaknesses; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14977v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">EnactOpt</a> jointly revises context construction and textual skills; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.20474v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">AdaRSI</a> diagnoses invocation before fine-tuning persistently unreliable substeps. Our assessment: navigation research should compare the costs and benefits of correcting evidence, scheduling, and policies; manipulation gains do not establish navigation gains.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This issue adds 175 records corresponding to 175 independent arXiv works, published between 2026-10-08 and 2026-10-15. Another 5 existing papers received version updates and are excluded from the new-work count. There are 0 new WeChat articles. Automatic primary-pool labels comprise 45 navigation and 19 embodied-agent works, totaling 64; the secondary pool comprises 90 VLA/manipulation, 7 autonomous-driving, and 14 other works, totaling 111. Labels have boundary errors: StereoEngine is classified as autonomous driving, Jev-Guard as embodied agents, and ASD and VP-EVT as other. Detailed analyses select directly relevant works from the primary pool; the categorized index follows actual content and covers all 175 works. Numbers below come from archived abstracts; missing splits, comparisons, and absolute scores are not inferred.</p>\n<h2 id=\"priority-reading\">2. Priority reading list</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14682v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">VLN-Safe</a></strong> · Collision evaluation on continuous R2R-CE\n    <ul>\n<li>Contribution: compares sliding and blocking collision handling and adds an independent collision guard.</li>\n<li>Evidence: switching to blocking reduces SR by 11.7–36.1 percentage points across 6 published agents.</li>\n<li>Why read: changes how existing navigation results and real-world transfer are interpreted.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.13122v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">GuidedNav</a></strong> · Representation supervision for continuous R2R-CE / RxR-CE\n    <ul>\n<li>Contribution: training-time supervision of active subtasks, landmark attention, and spatial representations.</li>\n<li>Evidence: the abstract states SR improvements of 8.1% / 13.1% on the respective val-unseen splits, without absolute SR; these are not reinterpreted as percentage points.</li>\n<li>Why read: strengthens instruction–action grounding while limiting online reasoning overhead.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.15782v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">RSI-Nav</a></strong> · A language–action data loop for continuous navigation\n    <ul>\n<li>Contribution: generates instructions, filters execution, and immediately updates the same model in shards.</li>\n<li>Evidence: R2R-CE SR 68.6%, RxR-CE SR 72.4%; splits are unspecified in the abstract.</li>\n<li>Why read: tests whether synthetic instructions improve in step with the current policy.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.18478v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">MemoryVLN</a></strong> · Long-term memory for continuous navigation\n    <ul>\n<li>Contribution: temporal compression, spatial anchoring, and confidence-controlled write-back.</li>\n<li>Evidence: the abstract reports R2R-CE / RxR-CE SR improvements of 2% / 6.5%, without absolute scores, splits, or clarification of relative versus absolute gains.</li>\n<li>Why read: separate the contributions of history compression and cross-context reuse.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.13104v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">VLN-AIEP</a></strong> · Instruction-execution progress\n    <ul>\n<li>Contribution: learns progress from navigation decision labels and suppresses executed instruction content.</li>\n<li>Evidence: evaluates discrete R2R, RxR, CVDN, and continuous R2R-CE; the abstract provides no verifiable metric values.</li>\n<li>Why read: contrasts with representation guidance requiring additional progress annotations.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.18307v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">I2O2I-Nav</a></strong> · Continuous indoor–outdoor–indoor missions\n    <ul>\n<li>Contribution: evaluates full missions from actual handoff states and localizes stage failures.</li>\n<li>Evidence: full-mission SR 1.15% → 4.02%, using the same underlying policies.</li>\n<li>Why read: stress-tests runtime handoffs and completion verification.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19431v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">S3-Nav</a></strong> · Separating evidence from execution authority\n    <ul>\n<li>Contribution: bounds decision context while retaining persistent spatial-semantic evidence and independent execution validation.</li>\n<li>Evidence: the abstract names neither environments nor verifiable numbers.</li>\n<li>Why read: relevant to navigation runtimes, but effectiveness requires full-paper verification.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19420v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">WEDGE</a></strong> · Failure-guided environment generation\n    <ul>\n<li>Contribution: validates weaknesses before changing the environmental factors that control them.</li>\n<li>Evidence: compares random and hard-example alternatives on ProcTHOR and reports HM3D transfer for some policies; the abstract gives no numbers.</li>\n<li>Why read: supports targeted ablations beyond merely increasing environment count.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"deep-analysis\">3. Detailed analyses</h2>\n<h3 id=\"vln-safe\">1. VLN-Safe: establish how collisions change actions</h3>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Reaching the goal does not imply collision-free motion. Sliding along obstacles can conceal unsafe actions’ consequences.</p>\n<p><strong>Method.</strong> The <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14682v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">paper</a> contrasts sliding and blocking on the same navigation task, using depth geometry and recent motion to detect risk. A guard vetoes risky forward actions, escapes, and returns control.</p>\n<p><strong>Evidence.</strong> Under R2R-CE blocking, the guard reduces collision steps by 62–98% and raises SR by 6.9–29.3 percentage points across 5 evaluated agents. These are cross-agent ranges, not one model’s score.</p>\n<p><strong>Value.</strong> Our assessment: prioritize replication because it tests reliance on sliding without first retraining the policy.</p>\n<p><strong>Limitations.</strong> Simulated collisions do not measure physical contact damage; the abstract does not establish guard reliability under sensor errors.</p>\n<p><strong>Action.</strong> Fix policies and episodes, compare both collision regimes, and record collision-free success, interventions, and added distance.</p>\n<h3 id=\"guidednav\">2. GuidedNav: keep progress and geometry supervision in training</h3>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Action labels alone may teach correlations without reliably grounding the current instruction segment and its landmarks.</p>\n<p><strong>Method.</strong> The <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.13122v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">paper</a> guides pre-action attention and constrains representations with geometry, introducing Guided-R2R and Guided-RxR annotations.</p>\n<p><strong>Evidence.</strong> The abstract reports improvements over compared monocular RGB methods and faster inference, but no absolute SR, latency, hardware, or training cost.</p>\n<p><strong>Value.</strong> Our assessment: test whether structural supervision can replace extra deployment-time textual reasoning and geometric encoding.</p>\n<p><strong>Limitations.</strong> Additional annotations have costs; the abstract cannot disentangle annotation scale, supervision design, and model configuration.</p>\n<p><strong>Action.</strong> Fix backbone and data volume, ablate subtask, landmark, and spatial supervision separately, and measure closed-loop latency.</p>\n<h3 id=\"rsi-nav\">3. RSI-Nav: keep the data generator aligned with the policy</h3>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Labeling all trajectories with a fixed model can leave generated instructions misaligned with an evolving navigator.</p>\n<p><strong>Method.</strong> The <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.15782v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">paper</a> shares instruction generation and following within one model: generate language in shards, execute, filter by completion and path efficiency, and update immediately.</p>\n<p><strong>Evidence.</strong> Both continuous VLN benchmarks have explicit SR values, but the abstract omits splits, confidence intervals, and paired gains over a fixed generator.</p>\n<p><strong>Value.</strong> Our assessment: replicate synchronized data–policy updates rather than focusing only on synthetic corpus size.</p>\n<p><strong>Limitations.</strong> Self-generation and filtering may favor already manageable routes and omit difficult instructions. This is a risk to test, not an established paper finding.</p>\n<p><strong>Action.</strong> Fix the trajectory budget, compare one-pass annotation with shard-wise updates, and track long instructions, difficult routes, and instruction diversity.</p>\n<h3 id=\"memoryvln\">4. MemoryVLN: compressed history still needs spatial provenance</h3>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Long histories face context limits, redundant observations, and unstable spatial alignment over time.</p>\n<p><strong>Method.</strong> The <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.18478v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">paper</a> samples recent history densely and distant history sparsely, associating semantics, geometry, and pose. Hierarchical memory supports cross-context warm starts, selective retrieval, and confidence-controlled write-back.</p>\n<p><strong>Evidence.</strong> The abstract reports gains on both continuous VLN benchmarks, but omits retrieval and geometry-encoding costs and experimental isolation of cross-context memory.</p>\n<p><strong>Value.</strong> Our assessment: evidence of what was seen where better supports revisitation and repeated search than location-free summaries.</p>\n<p><strong>Limitations.</strong> Check boundaries between test environments, trajectories, and persistent memory before interpreting prior scene exposure as unseen-environment generalization.</p>\n<p><strong>Action.</strong> Separate within-episode and cross-task memory; fix the token budget and compare compression, spatial anchoring, and write-back.</p>\n<h3 id=\"i2o2i-nav\">5. I2O2I-Nav: primitive competence does not ensure full missions</h3>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Isolated stage evaluation misses accumulated endpoint errors, handoff failures, and incorrect completion decisions.</p>\n<p><strong>Method.</strong> The <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.18307v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">paper</a> builds physically executable composite scenes and starts each stage at its predecessor’s actual endpoint. A modular agent adds completion verification, handoffs, and bounded recovery.</p>\n<p><strong>Evidence.</strong> The benchmark includes 20 composite scenes and 1,374 evaluation episodes. Primitive SR is 40.5–76%, but full-mission SR remains only 4.02%.</p>\n<p><strong>Value.</strong> Our assessment: a more deployment-relevant diagnostic target for ground delivery robots than isolated indoor or outdoor arrival.</p>\n<p><strong>Limitations.</strong> Benchmark results are not real delivery success rates; low full-mission performance does not establish reliable deployment.</p>\n<p><strong>Action.</strong> Record stage-conditional success, handoff states, and false stops, especially at the final ObjectNav stage.</p>\n<h2 id=\"transferable-methods\">4. Transferable methods</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Manipulation memory: <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.12710v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">REMM</a> / <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.12793v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">GAMMA</a>.</strong> The former withdraws task conclusions whose supporting evidence changes; the latter audits memory writes and subgoals. Apply to navigation memory and completion checks, requiring object identity, locations, and evidence dependencies. Manipulation gains do not transfer directly.</li>\n<li><strong>Manipulation scheduling: <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21160v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">When to Ask for Help</a>.</strong> Separates trigger quality from slow-module intervention value. Apply to cloud–edge navigation budgets, including latency and harmful interventions rather than judging system benefit from warning accuracy alone.</li>\n<li><strong>Language evaluation: <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.16634v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Language2Drive</a>.</strong> Fixes the starting scene and changes only feasible instructions to test behavioral dependence on language. Transfer to turns, detours, and ordering, after establishing that both instructions are executable.</li>\n<li><strong>Aerial VLN: <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21450v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">X-Fly</a>.</strong> Learns recovery and stopping from policy-visited states. Applicable to ground action chunks, but OpenFly results do not establish R2R-CE performance; dynamics and stopping regions require redesign.</li>\n<li><strong>Memory evaluation: <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.12561v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">MeMoREval</a>.</strong> Accumulates experience before future requests are known, then measures memory utility and writing/reading costs. Useful for comparing text, frame, and scene-graph navigation memory; its requests without new observations differ from online navigation.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"categorized-index\">5. Categorized index</h2>\n<p>A denotes direct ground-navigation relevance; B denotes an explicit transferable mechanism; C denotes field observation or low relevance. These reading categories do not replace automatic library labels. Detailed analyses are not repeated; secondary works are grouped by theme.</p>\n<h3 id=\"ground-navigation\">5.1 Ground VLN / ObjectNav / semantic navigation</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14077v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Two-Stage Multi-modal Learning for Dynamic Audio-Visual Navigation</a></strong> (A): Dynamic audio-visual navigation aligns sound intensity with geometry for unheard sounds.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.12733v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Visual-Wi-Fi Navigation to Signal-Emitting Devices</a></strong> (A): ViFiNav combines motion-dependent Wi-Fi bearings with depth to locate small or occluded devices.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.13122v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">GuidedNav</a></strong> (A): Training-time landmark, progress, and spatial guidance; see detailed analysis.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.13104v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">VLN-AIEP</a></strong> (A): VLN-AIEP anchors trajectories to landmarks and suppresses already executed instruction segments.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14682v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">VLN-Safe</a></strong> (A): Collision handling changes the interpretation of success; see detailed analysis.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.15038v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Training-Free Multi-Agent Cooperative Embodied Search from Uncertain Vision-Language Priors</a></strong> (A): Treats language-model priors as hypotheses and jointly plans multi-robot search through graph optimization.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14634v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">VbotNav-0</a></strong> (A): Asynchronous cloud subgoals and edge trajectory policies connect indoor and outdoor routes.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.15956v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">ADAPT</a></strong> (A): DarkNav-Bench / ADAPT uses spatial audio and depth for navigation without visible illumination.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.15782v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">RSI-Nav</a></strong> (A): RSI-Nav updates instruction generation and navigation together in shards; see detailed analysis.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.15595v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">FireNav</a></strong> (A): Routes reconstruction evidence by navigation utility under fire-related visual degradation.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.15375v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">RefBeliefNav</a></strong> (A): RefBeliefNav separates target belief from evidence uncertainty and unifies exploration with revisitation.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.15362v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">LERR-Nav</a></strong> (A): Learns when to explore, recover, and revisit historical cues.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.16411v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Estimating the Causal Impact of Social Navigation Signals in Corridor Scenarios</a></strong> (A): Observational associations of social-navigation signals can oppose their intervention effects.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.16558v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">G2WAM</a></strong> (A): G2WAM anchors history and goal images in shared coordinates and jointly predicts latent states and actions.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.17089v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">MultINav</a></strong> (A): MultINav models interaction-driven changes in connectivity or target visibility and verifies graph updates.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.16103v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">PathLift</a></strong> (A): Uses sensor depth to correct unitless navigation trajectories waypoint by waypoint.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.18321v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">CoSMO</a></strong> (A): Uses calibrated category sets for semantic safety clearance; coverage guarantees do not imply zero collisions.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.18373v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">DR-VLA</a></strong> (A): Transfers human egocentric video to quadruped navigation; abstract evidence is mainly pseudo-closed-loop and open-loop.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.18307v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">I2O2I-Nav</a></strong> (A): Evaluates actual indoor–outdoor stage handoffs; see detailed analysis.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.18093v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">LynxScenes</a></strong> (A): Outdoor wheel-legged multisensor data with pseudo-closed-loop evaluation; data and tools are planned for release.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.18478v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">MemoryVLN</a></strong> (A): Spatial anchoring and cross-context memory reuse; see detailed analysis.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.18135v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">SkipWorld</a></strong> (A): Filters trajectories with latent skip predictions, then reranks them with an autoregressive model.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.17593v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">ASD</a></strong> (A): ASD distills language goals and navigation reasoning from privileged training-time trajectory feedback.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19600v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Cracking Mobile Manipulation</a></strong> (A): Diagnoses mobile manipulation through planning, base positioning, and local manipulation before targeted intervention.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19618v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">F2A-ObjectNav</a></strong> (A): Event-driven target validation, candidate management, recovery, and strategy switching.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19420v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">WEDGE</a></strong> (A): WEDGE validates policy weaknesses and generates targeted training environments.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.20121v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">HM3D-M3</a></strong> (A): HM3D-M3 combines multiple robots, goal modalities, and object goals within one episode.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.20998v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">MoCaP</a></strong> (A): MoCaP extracts spatial memory and priors for manipulation-ready base configurations from human video.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19431v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">S3-Nav</a></strong> (A): Separates persistent state, decision evidence, and execution authority; effectiveness needs full-paper checks.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19361v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">VLX-Go</a></strong> (A): Uses staged learning and policy-visited-state refinement for following amid distractors.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21586v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">AVALON</a></strong> (A): Seeds topological memory from user floor plans and place clues, with updates verified in situ.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21429v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">MuSTMM</a></strong> (A): Cross-scene sound-triggered mobile manipulation uses pose-aligned depth as navigation memory.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21619v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Training-Free Target-Aware Refinement of 3D Gaussian Language Map</a></strong> (A): Minimally injects target priors into existing language maps while suppressing query-independent bias.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21999v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">VP-EVT</a></strong> (A): Makes target visibility a motion objective to actively resolve persistent occlusion.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"memory-planning\">5.2 Memory, maps, planning, and evaluation</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.13849v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">HorizonNav</a></strong> (B): Reconstructs plannable space from generated video; imagined feasibility does not establish real traversability.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14977v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">EnactOpt</a></strong> (B): Jointly evolves context-construction code and textual skills with fixed models and runtime.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14635v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">PVFH-Bench</a></strong> (B): Diagnoses visual hallucinations induced by false textual premises; relevant to absent-target navigation tests.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14601v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">StateSkill-Agent</a></strong> (B): StateSkill organizes skills and stage-relevant context as finite-state machines.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.16895v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Observe, Share, or Predict?</a></strong> (B): PARTNR compares observation, knowledge sharing, and action prediction; more prediction need not improve cooperation.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.17651v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Code2Worlds-2</a></strong> (B): Compiles physical properties from visual scenes, then simulates, diagnoses, and refines executable environments.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.17640v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">MemAct</a></strong> (B): MemAct combines memory writing, historical retrieval, and active viewpoint acquisition in one loop.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.18792v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">SEAR</a></strong> (B): Separates retained-experience gains from retries, budget, and task familiarity.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19108v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Towards a Data-Centric Understanding of Spatial Reasoning in Multimodal Large Language Models</a></strong> (B): Surveys spatial-reasoning data construction and evaluation, emphasizing supervision and metric definitions.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.17657v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">SCOPER</a></strong> (B): SCOPER jointly examines evidence memory, execution progress, and failure recovery.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.20474v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">AdaRSI</a></strong> (B): Repairs skill invocation before fine-tuning the executor on persistently failing substeps.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19523v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Dungeon and Visibility World Generator</a></strong> (B): Generates controllable geometry and visibility for exploration evaluation.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.20409v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">C-Slerp</a></strong> (B): C-Slerp composes intermediate goals in spherical representations and adapts waypoint count to difficulty.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.20560v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">PhySGA</a></strong> (B): Verifies scene composition with physics feedback, informing executability checks in environment generation.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.20312v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">HiMeco</a></strong> (B): HiMeco separates perception, exploration, and planning failures before retaining execution-validated skills.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.20442v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Towards Agentic Robots</a></strong> (B): Surveys agentic robotics across models, data, environments, and harnesses.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21783v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">ACT3D</a></strong> (B): ACT3D learns viewpoint selection from answer outcomes; spatial-QA accuracy is not navigation success.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.12793v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">GAMMA</a></strong> (B): GAMMA separates memory writing and reasoning, auditing claims against mechanical visual evidence.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.12561v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">MeMoREval</a></strong> (B): MeMoREval accumulates experience before future tasks are known, comparing memory modalities and inference costs.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.13021v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">EmbodiedCLBench</a></strong> (B): EmbodiedCLBench tests whether basic-task experience supports unseen skill compositions.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.12957v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">JoyInside-VLM-Agent</a></strong> (B): Product-conditioned multimodal policy training includes goal revisions and execution feedback.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14302v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">DySCOVER</a></strong> (B): DySCOVER uses prediction error to guide active collection of robot visual-learning experience.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.13534v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">MAGMA-Bench</a></strong> (B): MAGMA-Bench evaluates interruptions, execution failures, and heterogeneous robot coordination at a skill interface.</li>\n<li><strong><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22322v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">MPT</a></strong> (B): MPT jointly learns bidding-based participation and action policies for decentralized coordination.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"vla-manipulation\">5.3 Embodied VLA / mobile manipulation</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Manipulation memory, skills, and runtimes (C)</strong>：<a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14106v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Embodied Zero-Shot Task Planning via Hardware-Aware LLM-VLM Integration for Grounded Robotic Manipulation</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.12710v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">REMM</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.13262v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Dual-Stream Memory for Vision-Language-Action Policies</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14406v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Memory Without Curation</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.15666v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">A Persian Vision-Language-Action Dataset and Benchmark for Ambiguity- and Infeasibility-Aware Instruction-to-Plan Grounding</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.15425v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Exploring the Limits of Combinatorial Generalization in Embodied Manipulation</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Manipulation memory, skills, and runtimes (C)</strong>：<a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.15766v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">SurgiCLAW</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.17350v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Can a Frontier VLM Serve as a Robot Policy for Memory-Dependent Manipulation?</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.18874v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">ChunkAct</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.18790v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">EqC</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.18650v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">ForkCast</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.18352v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Let the Mind Conceive, Let the Hand Achieve</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Manipulation memory, skills, and runtimes (C)</strong>：<a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19677v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">ACRO</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19579v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Back on Track</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19495v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Robota-Embed</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19517v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">VLRLA</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21453v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">CarryOn</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22070v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Memory as Weights</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Manipulation memory, skills, and runtimes (C)</strong>：<a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22178v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">REKI</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Manipulation evaluation, instructions, and preferences (C)</strong>：<a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.12696v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Spa-Bench</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.13628v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">JitterVLA</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14415v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Red-Teaming of Vision-Language-Action Models via Quality Diversity Environment Generation</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.16782v2\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">LIBERO-Industry</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.16567v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">VLA-GAUGE</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.18843v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">LIBERO-RIG</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Manipulation evaluation, instructions, and preferences (C)</strong>：<a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.18869v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">What Matters in Quality-Aware VLA Pretraining</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19438v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Paraphrase Consensus Is Not Self-Consistency</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21251v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Do VLAs Really Follow Instructions? Diagnosing How-Level Instruction Following in Vision-Language-Action Models</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22372v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">LIBERO-PeRM</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Manipulation data, transfer, and policy optimization (C)</strong>：<a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.12734v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">StereoEngine</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14155v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Axiom-0</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.12861v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Evidence-Guided Counterfactual Learning for Long-Tailed Robotic Policies</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.13943v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Noise Space Learning for VLA Adaptation</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.12914v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">OGPO</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.13578v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">RARA</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Manipulation data, transfer, and policy optimization (C)</strong>：<a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.13945v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Splat2Act</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.12832v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">VLMs are Native Action Models</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14936v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">CFG-Policy</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14588v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Flow-Support-Weighted Proposals for Test-Time Verified VLA Policies</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14885v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Verify, Act, Distill</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.15337v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">CoLA</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Manipulation data, transfer, and policy optimization (C)</strong>：<a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.16517v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Trust-Calibrated VLA Policy Post-Training with Evolving Imagination</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.16969v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">UniLAP</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.17983v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Breaking the Average</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19143v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Post-Training, Finetuning, and Steering</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.20504v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Graph-Reweighted Behavior Cloning with Human Interventions for Precise Robotic Manipulation</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.20351v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Human Demonstrations as Post-Training Data for Any Robot Body</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Manipulation data, transfer, and policy optimization (C)</strong>：<a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.20767v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Multi-Dimensional Featurizers Yield Fine-Grained Steering and Data Shaping in Robot Foundation Models</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19746v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">One-shot Human Video-Guided Adaptation for Vision-Language-Action Models</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21522v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Multi-Teacher On-Policy Distillation for Flow-Based Vision-Language-Action Models</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Future prediction and action representations (C)</strong>：<a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.13212v2\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Beyond Actions</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.12936v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Evo-World</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.13117v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Forward Dynamics Models</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.15556v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Vision-Predictor-Action</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.15794v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Delta Flow Matching</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.16160v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">FlowVLA</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Future prediction and action representations (C)</strong>：<a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.18774v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">GreenWAM</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.18568v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">WAVE-0</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.17922v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">WoVR+</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.20840v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">A Phenomenology of Neural Geometry in Vision-Language-Action Models</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19753v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Coordi-pi</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.20223v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">ObjDream</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Future prediction and action representations (C)</strong>：<a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19825v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">What Matters for World Modeling in Vision-Language-Action Models?</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21922v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">TS-WAM</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21306v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">VLA-Delta</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21915v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">What Should Robot Policies Learn to Predict? Rethinking Supervision Targets in Vision-Language-Action Learning</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Precision manipulation, multimodality, and body control (C)</strong>：<a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14854v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">DEFT-VLA</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14848v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Learning Dynamic Grasping</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14704v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">PRISM</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.15675v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Recovering Low-Bit Failures in Vision-Language-Action Policies via Quantization-Conditioned Reinforcement Learning</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.16313v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Listen Before Act</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.18855v2\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Interaction Latents as Actions for Contact-Rich Manipulation</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Precision manipulation, multimodality, and body control (C)</strong>：<a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.17540v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">TacitVLA</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.17999v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">TRACE</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.20750v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Causal Analysis of Robot Foundation Models Enables Efficiency Gains</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19604v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">ForceBench</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.20064v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">FoveaVLA</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19651v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">SPECTRA-VLA</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Precision manipulation, multimodality, and body control (C)</strong>：<a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21152v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">KODA</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Inference compression, action chunks, and verification (C)</strong>：<a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19656v2\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Accelerating VLA Inference with Perturbation Informed Execution</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21389v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Fast-VLA — You Only Think Once</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22146v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">FastVLA — Adaptive Visual Token Compression</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21434v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Policy-Aligned Predictive Verification for Adaptive VLA Action-Chunk Execution</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21985v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">VeriVLA</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21160v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">When to Ask for Help</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"lower-relevance\">5.4 Aerial, driving, and lower-relevance directions</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Aerial navigation and interaction (C)</strong>：<a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.12761v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Verifier-Mediated Multi-LLM Coordination for Multi-Robot Path Planning</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14702v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">BGC-Nav</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.17419v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">ClearNav</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.18381v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Benchmarking Interaction-Oriented Aerial Mobility across Heterogeneous Environments</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21978v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Towards bio-inspired visual navigation</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21450v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">X-Fly</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Geometric control, path planning, and estimation (C)</strong>：<a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.13007v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">CeDR-Nav</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.12877v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">TAME-Nav</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14596v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Load-Aware Quadruped Navigation with Online Contact-based Geometry Estimation</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.16522v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Learning Reachability-Based Safety Representations for Real-Time Robot Navigation in Unknown and Dynamic Environments</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.17232v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">LiDAR-based Humanoid Navigation in Dynamic, Non-Convex Environments via Spatio-Temporal Safe Corridors</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19224v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">End-to-End Vision-Language-Action Policies for Autonomous Lunar Rover Docking</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Geometric control, path planning, and estimation (C)</strong>：<a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.18186v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Reinforcement Learning for Large-Scale Lifelong Multi-Agent Path Finding with Shared Spatial Fields</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19044v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">RingNav</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21086v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">RoPE-Flow</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21222v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Vision-Guided Closed-Loop Autonomous Navigation of Light-Driven Janus Microbots</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Driving and motion-based visual adaptation (C)</strong>：<a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.13453v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Reflect-VLA</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14802v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">YOUDrive</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.15846v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Mobility Helps Learning</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.16985v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">AUGUstus</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.16634v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Language2Drive</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.20609v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">SafeDriveVLA</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>General agents, non-navigation work, and retrieval noise (C)</strong>：<a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.13056v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">A4-MR</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14431v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">MemForm</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.18326v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Jev-Guard</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.18617v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Two Routes to Interoceptive Prioritisation</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.20486v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Average-Reward Policy Identification from a Single Continuing Trajectory</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19423v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Beyond the Trial-and-Error Loop</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>General agents, non-navigation work, and retrieval noise (C)</strong>：<a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19726v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Generative World Simulation with DAO and JING</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19920v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">MolHop</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21484v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Context Matters! Social, Spatial, and Semantic Coordination with an Embodied Agent in Immersive Analytics</a>; <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21604v1\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Evaluating Inquiry Learning in LLM-Based Agents</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"news\">5.5 News and non-paper items</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>No new WeChat news this issue. All items above are arXiv records, including surveys, benchmarks, dissertations, and other research formats.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"trends-and-actions\">6. Trends and recommended actions</h2>\n<h3 id=\"trends\">Trends</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Execution verification is becoming a separate research target.</strong> VLN-Safe, S3-Nav, and I2O2I-Nav expose collision, evidence-authority, and handoff problems. Our assessment: measure correct proposals, permission to execute, and actual completion separately.</li>\n<li><strong>Memory increasingly connects to active evidence acquisition.</strong> MemoryVLN, RefBeliefNav, and MemAct address reuse, revisitation, and new viewpoints. Our assessment: measure repeated search avoided and incorrect beliefs withdrawn.</li>\n<li><strong>Data and system improvements need failure attribution.</strong> WEDGE, EnactOpt, and AdaRSI intervene on environments, runtimes, and executors. Our assessment: the automatic primary pool contains 64 of 175 works; a large secondary pool does not mean manipulation dominates relevant progress. Allocate reading by research question, not paper count.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"research-gaps\">Research gaps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Abstract-level SR gains do not establish how cross-context memory handles scene changes, conflicting evidence, and test-information isolation.</li>\n<li>Continuous indoor–outdoor missions need joint evaluation of stopping, handoffs, and collision regimes; primitive scores cannot predict full-mission reliability.</li>\n<li>Direct comparisons of added supervision, memory, and guards under matched hardware, sensors, interaction, and training budgets remain missing.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"recommended-actions\">Recommended actions</h3>\n<p><strong>High priority</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Replicate VLN-Safe’s evaluation comparison.</strong> Establish whether local R2R-CE uses sliding, then evaluate blocking and the collision guard.</li>\n<li><strong>Read GuidedNav, RSI-Nav, MemoryVLN, and VLN-AIEP closely.</strong> Record splits, baselines, absolute SR/SPL, gain definitions, and compute budgets before comparing them.</li>\n<li><strong>Add stage-handoff logging.</strong> Following I2O2I-Nav, record predecessor endpoints, successor readiness, recovery, and evidence for final stopping.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Medium priority</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Track S3-Nav, WEDGE, and RefBeliefNav.</strong> Design small ablations for execution authorization, difficult-environment generation, and uncertainty-guided revisitation.</li>\n<li><strong>Check transferable memory protocols.</strong> Compare MeMoREval and REMM using invalidated evidence, withdrawal of false completion, and reobservation tasks.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Low priority</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Defer wholesale adoption of manipulation, aerial-navigation, and general-agent systems.</strong> Validate specific interfaces or evaluation mechanisms first; do not extrapolate cross-task success into ground VLN gains.</li>\n</ul>\n", "styles": ["\n  /* 回到顶部按钮样式 */\n  #backToTop {\n    position: fixed;\n    bottom: 2rem;\n    right: 2rem;\n    width: 3rem;\n    height: 3rem;\n    background: var(--theme-color, #a4a8b1);\n    color: white;\n    border: none;\n    border-radius: 50%;\n    cursor: pointer;\n    opacity: 0;\n    transform: translateY(100%);\n    transition: all 0.3s ease;\n    box-shadow: 0 4px 6px -1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1);\n    display: flex;\n    align-items: center;\n    justify-content: center;\n    z-index: 999; /* 确保在其它元素之上 */\n  }\n  \n  #backToTop.show {\n    opacity: 1;\n    transform: translateY(0);\n  }\n  \n  @media (hover: hover) {\n    #backToTop:hover {\n      background: var(--theme-hover-color, #a4a8b1);\n      transform: translateY(0) scale(1.1);\n    }\n  }\n  "], "articleClasses": ["posts", "paper-figures"], "removedImages": 0};
